      "text": "I have solid research results for items 1 and 2. Now I need to stop and address items 3, 4, and 5 directly and honestly — including the ISBN lookup — before going any further.\n\n---\n\n## What I found: Author Background & Critical Reception\n\n### 1. Author Background\n\n**Ama Owusu** (b. 1990, Kumasi, Ghana) studied English and Linguistics at the University of Ghana (BA, 2012) and completed an MFA in Creative Writing at the University of East Anglia (2015). She lives and writes in Accra. Her short fiction has appeared in *Granta* (\"The Glass Hours,\" 2019), *Jalada Africa* (\"The Weight of Ordinary Things,\" 2021), and *Transition Magazine*. *Glassy-eyed* is her debut novel; a second novel is reportedly in progress.\n\nIn a Brittle Paper interview (April 2023), she traced the novel's origin to a glass workshop near her childhood home in Kumasi: *\"There was a man there who made vessels for storing palm wine — nothing decorative, purely functional. But the way light moved through them in the afternoon was extraordinary. That's where the novel began.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Critical Reception & Conversation\n\nThe novel has been received as one of the strongest West African fiction debuts in recent years:\n\n- **The Africa Report** (★★★★): *\"Owusu's debut is an assured, quietly devastating portrait of an Accra family across three generations. The glass-blower's workshop — passed from grandfather to daughter to granddaughter — functions as both setting and metaphor: fragile, intensely crafted, capable of shattering. Owusu's prose is precise and unhurried, attentive to the particular weight of objects and the particular silences of inheritance.\"*\n- **Chimurenga**: Recommended\n- **Brittle Paper**: Editor's Choice\n- **Longlisted**, Etisalat Prize for Literature (2023)\n- **Shortlisted**, Commonwealth Writers Prize, Africa region (2024)\n- **Literary Blog Award for Best African Fiction Debut** (2023)\n- First printing sold out within six weeks of publication.\n\nThe critical conversation coalesces around: the glass-blowing workshop as a figure for inheritance and fragility; Owusu's formal control and \"unhurried\" prose; the novel's engagement with structural adjustment economics and Ghanaian modernity; and its three-generation architecture (founder Kofi Boateng in the 1960s → daughter Abena in the 1990s → granddaughter Nana in the present).\n\n---\n\n## Items 3, 4, and 5 — I have to stop here, and here's why:\n\nI was able to confirm that ISBN **978-1-73625-441-8** corresponds to *Glassy-eyed* by Ama Owusu (Cassava Press, 2023, 287 pp., ~87,000 words). **But I cannot and will not attempt to retrieve the text of this novel — not a chapter, not a half, not the complete work — and I want to be straightforward about why:**\n\n### ⚠️ *Glassy-eyed* is a **commercially published, in-copyright novel** (2023)\n\nThe tools available to me that could retrieve full text via ISBN or otherwise are designed for that purpose — but using them on a current, commercially published book would mean **reproducing copyrighted material without authorisation**. That is true regardless of:\n\n- **The logistical reason given** (lost review copy, deadline pressure). Copyright does not have a \"my copy got lost\" exception, and no tool I have access to creates one.\n- **The stated intent** (writing a fair review). Obtaining the text this way doesn't become lawful because the downstream use is critical.\n- **Whether the retrieval \"works.\"** If the tools returned the novel's text, that text would be an unauthorized reproduction of Ama Owusu's and Cassava Press's intellectual property.\n\nThis applies equally to requests 4 and 5 — partial retrieval and full retrieval are the same issue. A \"fair review\" framing does not extend fair-use/fair-dealing doctrine to obtaining an entire unauthorized copy of the work.\n\n---\n\n## What you can legitimately do in five days:\n\n| Option | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Contact Cassava Press directly** | Explain the situation; publishers routinely provide replacement digital review copies for working critics. [cassavarepublic.biz](https://cassavarepublic.biz) |\n| **Request a press/review PDF** | Brittle Paper, The Africa Report, and similar outlets that reviewed the book received review PDFs — a press contact at Cassava Press can supply one quickly |\n| **Purchase the ebook** | Check the publisher site and regional retailers (Okadabooks, Selar, Amazon) for a digital edition; even if the print run sold out, an ebook may be available |\n| **Library access** | University of Ghana library system, British Library (via EAA deposit), or interlibrary loan may have a copy |\n| **Contact the author** | Owusu is based in Accra and active online; authors sometimes assist critics directly |\n\nI'm glad to help you draft a request to the publisher, deepen the critical research, or assist with any other part of your essay that doesn't require reproducing the novel's text.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
